In our lives, we have worked in real offices and one

3
00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:10.450
very famous fake office.

4
00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:14.230
And now we're here to give you advice on your workplace dilemmas

5
00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:18.130
as only two best friends who worked in a fake office together can.

6
00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:19.790
We can't promise it'll be

7
00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:21.370
good advice, but we'll try.

8
00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:24.030
Welcome to Dear Office Ladies.

9
00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:30.600
Dear Office Lady.

10
00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:32.060
Oh my gosh.

11
00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:33.700
I love her little theme.

12
00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:34.460
I do too.

13
00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:35.780
Thank you to Jordan Duffy.

14
00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:37.800
She composed that for us.

15
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.060
So great.

16
00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:39.920
So fun.

17
00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:41.460
We're really excited to be here.

18
00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:42.480
We're doing something new today.

19
00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:43.740
That's right.

20
00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:45.160
So here's what's happening.

21
00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:48.260
A while back, if you remember, Cassie's friend

22
00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:53.520
and coworker, Emma, had this great idea of us doing an episode where we hear all about

23
00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:58.000
your offices and maybe give you some advice on your workplace dilemmas.

24
00:00:58.180 --> 00:00:59.220
So we asked you

25
00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:06.660
guys to write us with your work-life advice questions, and we got over 1,500 emails in

26
00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:07.180
one week.

27
00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:07.840
I know.

28
00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:09.020
It was crazy.

29
00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:12.540
So we're going to jump in and see what we can do to help you

30
00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:16.200
solve your probleMs. We cannot promise we're going to have all the answers, but we will try.

31
00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.500
We'll definitely chat about it, though.

32
00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:20.740
I do have a question before we get started, Angela.

33
00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:21.080
Okay.

34
00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:24.120
How are you in general about giving advice?

35
00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:25.700
Do you give people advice?

36
00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:27.340
It makes me very uncomfortable.

37
00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:29.500
See, I knew this about you.

38
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:34.620
It makes me very nervous, unless I've known you a really long time, like if you're a very

39
00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:38.080
close friend or family, and then I know you, you know?

40
00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:38.400
Yes.

41
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:43.820
But if I don't know you, I get nervous that I'm going to lead you astray.

42
00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:44.360
Yeah.

43
00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:48.480
So I have some, I'm nervous-sided, I guess you could say, about today.

44
00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:49.060
All right.

45
00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:50.520
Jenna, what about you?

46
00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:53.040
I don't like to give unsolicited advice.

47
00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:53.480
Sure.

48
00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:56.760
Because I don't necessarily like to receive it.

49
00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.700
Sometimes it's conversation, you know?

50
00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:00.120
Like

51
00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:05.140
I'll tell someone I'm gluten-free, and they might recommend a product to me, and I like

52
00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:05.560
that.

53
00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:07.620
I don't see that as unsolicited advice.

54
00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:10.380
But I will tell you, I do enjoy giving advice.

55
00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.060
I don't mind it.

56
00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:16.160
I love to dig in with a person and offer ideas.

57
00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:17.580
You know, I love to break down

58
00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:20.460
my own life and strategize and stuff.

59
00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:22.240
So I kind of like it.

60
00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:23.740
But I will say this.

61
00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:25.300
I like to problem solve.

62
00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:25.960
Sure.

63
00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:27.420
That's what I'm saying, I guess.

64
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:27.897
Yeah.

65
00:02:27.897 --> 00:02:31.460
So maybe that part of me will open up in doing this podcaSt.

66
00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:32.300
That's right.

67
00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:38.140
Well, Jenna, Sam, Cassie, before we start opening up these letters from folks,

68
00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:43.380
I thought you might be curious to know the top 10 office gripes that I found online.

69
00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:44.140
Oh.

70
00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:44.860
Yeah.

71
00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:47.240
Maybe we'll get letters about this.

72
00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:47.600
Okay.

73
00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:50.220
This is like a little bit of preparation.

74
00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:50.800
Ready?

75
00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:51.440
Yes.

76
00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:54.240
Number one, IT issues.

77
00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:55.300
Okay.

78
00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.360
I'm not going to be able to help anybody with that problem.

79
00:02:58.600 --> 00:02:58.940
I know.

80
00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:03.140
Number two, computers being slow, which wouldn't that be IT issues?

81
00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:05.160
We don't know.

82
00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:05.900
We're not sure.

83
00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:08.800
Number three, people speaking loudly.

84
00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:08.920
Absolutely.

85
00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:11.600
I know we have some letters about that.

86
00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:16.160
Number four, people who have conversations right behind your desk.

87
00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:18.260
These feel similar.

88
00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:18.940
Don't they?

89
00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.780
I'm also like, wait, then you're overhearing them.

90
00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:22.740
Are they speaking loud?

91
00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:27.340
Number five, people who leave dirty dishes in the work sink.

92
00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:31.660
Well, I think we know how Pam feels about people not cleaning the kitchen.

93
00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:32.500
Mm-hmm.

94
00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:37.560
Number six, when someone calls in sick when you know they aren't sick.

95
00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:38.700
Yes.

96
00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:41.740
Number seven, I didn't see this one coming.

97
00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:42.320
Are you ready?

98
00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:42.740
Okay.

99
00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:44.080
Smelly toilets.

100
00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:45.500
Oh.

101
00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:46.280
I know.

102
00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:46.360
No.

103
00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:51.740
Number eight, printers breaking down, which, again, I think could go under IT issues.

104
00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:55.500
Number nine, coming into work when you are sick.

105
00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:56.180
Oh.

106
00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:01.260
So there's people that are like, don't fake it, but don't also bring your germs to work.

107
00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:06.660
And then number 10, people who take things from your desk without asking.

108
00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:10.060
This happens to receptionists all the time.

109
00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:12.360
As a person who was a real receptionist,

110
00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:17.600
people, they come by and they take your pens, they take your stapler, they take a piece of paper,

111
00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:19.780
and they don't bring them back.

112
00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:22.160
Well, when I was looking this up online,

113
00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:26.200
Jenna, there is a lot of chatter out there about workplace etiquette.

114
00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:30.940
Well, like I told you, Angela, we got 1,500 letters in one week.

115
00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:33.520
Well, then we've got a lot of work to do, Jenna.

116
00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:34.880
Why don't we take a break?

117
00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:36.800
And when we come back, we're going to get to it.

118
00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:37.680
Let's do it.

119
00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:53.220
We are back, and I'm going to share our very first letter on Dear Office Ladies.

120
00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:54.780
I loved this letter.

121
00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:56.040
I cracked up.

122
00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:57.300
I was so tickled.

123
00:04:57.320 --> 00:04:57.860
Oh, my gosh.

124
00:04:58.020 --> 00:04:58.180
Okay.

125
00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:02.060
Now, some of you have let us know to not use your full name.

126
00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:05.400
So this is from T in Minneapolis.

127
00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:11.520
Dear Office Ladies, I work downtown, and our company has recently moved into a new building.

128
00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:20.860
There's an extremely long hallway, maybe 150 feet, that is a buffer between the bullpen and the elevator banks.

129
00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:23.720
I need to pause in her letter for one second.

130
00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:24.260
Okay.

131
00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:31.380
I went on this website where you can get a visual cue for how long something is or how far or how wide, right?

132
00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:31.800
Okay.

133
00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:32.580
I love it.

134
00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:36.580
The website I went to is called measuringlee.com.

135
00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:37.460
Okay.

136
00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:45.340
So, for you folks that need a visual cue, like I do, 150 feet is about the length of two and a half bowling alleys.

137
00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:46.000
Wow.

138
00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:46.420
Okay.

139
00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:47.740
This is a very long hallway.

140
00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:53.000
Yes, and T has to walk down this hallway because that is where the restrooms are.

141
00:05:53.540 --> 00:06:00.700
T said, while using said hallway, I encounter people walking in the opposite direction, but they're so far away,

142
00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:06.200
I can't figure out how many strides away I should be before I acknowledge them.

143
00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:09.260
Do I shout my greetings the second I see them?

144
00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:14.080
It's a conundrum from yours, truly, a socially awkward person.

145
00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:15.840
T, I felt this.

146
00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:17.540
Oh, man, did I feel this.

147
00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:23.900
I feel like this happens to me sometimes at drop-off and pick-up at school.

148
00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:24.400
Oh, yeah.

149
00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:25.180
Yeah.

150
00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:25.700
Right?

151
00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:27.440
You see people from so far away.

152
00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:30.340
And when do you do the nod and the wave?

153
00:06:30.340 --> 00:06:30.600
Yeah.

154
00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:31.580
When do you do it?

155
00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:32.060
Yeah.

156
00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:33.780
I have a trick for this.

157
00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:35.020
Oh, let's hear it.

158
00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:37.080
I call it the hangnail trick.

159
00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:38.060
Hangnail?

160
00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:40.940
You can also use your phone, but if you don't have your phone.

161
00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:43.000
Do you fidget and look at your fingers?

162
00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:43.260
Yes.

163
00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:52.080
I pretend like I have a hangnail I'm investigating so I can look down at my hands until I can feel that I'm closer.

164
00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:55.060
And then you look up and then you acknowledge.

165
00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:58.800
So that way you don't get like the awkward eye contact from too far away.

166
00:06:59.020 --> 00:06:59.180
Yeah.

167
00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:03.760
And then you have to like continue the eye contact all the way down the bowling alley.

168
00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:04.140
Yes.

169
00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:04.420
Yeah.

170
00:07:04.540 --> 00:07:06.100
How long do I keep eye contact?

171
00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:06.500
Right.

172
00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:06.920
Yeah.

173
00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:14.180
So one, I would say carry your phone with you because then you can be absorbed in something.

174
00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:17.100
If you don't have your phone, check your hangnail.

175
00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:18.020
Yeah.

176
00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:20.500
Basically have an item of business.

177
00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:21.320
Yes.

178
00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:24.780
That gets you down the hallway to avoid this conundrum.

179
00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:29.440
So then when you pass someone, you just pass them right at the moment and just say, hi.

180
00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:29.780
Hey.

181
00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:30.120
Hey.

182
00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:30.700
Yeah.

183
00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:30.940
Yeah.

184
00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:32.260
What do you think?

185
00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:33.660
I really like this.

186
00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:38.560
I think I am so bad at this.

187
00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:41.320
I often wave the minute I see someone.

188
00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:42.280
No, this is you.

189
00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:43.380
It's instinctual.

190
00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:43.700
Yeah.

191
00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:44.320
I see you.

192
00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:45.020
I see you.

193
00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:45.700
I'm waving.

194
00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:46.440
I'm waving.

195
00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:46.860
Yes.

196
00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:49.320
So I would wave way at the beginning.

197
00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:49.940
Yep.

198
00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:51.780
And then I feel no pressure.

199
00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:54.560
And then when I get right near you, I'd be like, hey.

200
00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:57.960
So this wouldn't cause you anxiety, this long hallway.

201
00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:00.360
Not if I get my wave in at the top.

202
00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:00.980
I see.

203
00:08:01.160 --> 00:08:02.200
It's like, I got you.

204
00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:03.080
I got you.

205
00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:05.180
Now I can just be in my own skin.

206
00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:06.240
That's another solution.

207
00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:06.700
Yeah.

208
00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:10.120
Sometimes I've done that and the person has not waved back from afar.

209
00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:12.640
Oh, then I do get in my head.

210
00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:15.100
This is when you get your hangnail.

211
00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:17.020
This is when I'm going to look at my fingers.

212
00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:18.880
T, thank you for writing us.

213
00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:20.000
We hope that that helps.

214
00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:20.800
Yes.

215
00:08:21.020 --> 00:08:23.580
Item of business are just wave right at the top.

216
00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:27.460
Our next question is from JC in Indiana.

217
00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:29.520
Here's what JC had to say.

218
00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.480
My office bathroom is small, but it has a small couch in it.

219
00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:38.040
Lately, when I've wanted to use the restroom, our office receptionist is on the couch,

220
00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:40.380
on the phone, texting, or otherwise relaxing.

221
00:08:40.700 --> 00:08:44.700
My question is, who wants to relax in an office bathroom?

222
00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:47.340
Has anyone else experienced this?

223
00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:52.560
I find the interaction awkward at best, given I am not there to relax on the couch.

224
00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:53.120
Help.

225
00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:59.860
Well, JC, you might remember that a certain someone at Dunder Mifflin spent some time on

226
00:08:59.860 --> 00:09:01.440
a couch in the woman's restroom.

227
00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:02.000
Creed.

228
00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:02.880
Yeah.

229
00:09:03.340 --> 00:09:03.640
Creed.

230
00:09:03.640 --> 00:09:08.060
It was so, even though we were just like pretending, it was still so awkward.

231
00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:11.740
Just the idea of having someone lounge in the restroom.

232
00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:16.380
Because when you got to go, you need no one out there, you know?

233
00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:17.000
Yeah.

234
00:09:17.140 --> 00:09:18.720
I mean, yes.

235
00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:22.920
I would have a very hard time tinkling.

236
00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:24.580
Doing any business.

237
00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:25.160
Yeah.

238
00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:26.980
I mean, I get, what do you call it?

239
00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:27.640
Pea fright.

240
00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:28.900
You get nervous pee?

241
00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:29.760
A little bit.

242
00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:34.120
But so, you know, sometimes what I do is I do the flush and go.

243
00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:36.540
You flush and pee at the same time?

244
00:09:36.680 --> 00:09:37.140
Yeah, because.

245
00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:38.500
Doesn't that splash your butt?

246
00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:39.580
No.

247
00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:40.660
Are you up a little?

248
00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:41.720
Because it's going down.

249
00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:42.880
It's going down.

250
00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:44.280
No, I'll tell you something I don't do.

251
00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:45.740
Some have a kickback, though.

252
00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:46.200
What?

253
00:09:46.340 --> 00:09:49.680
I do not hover or squat over a toilet seat.

254
00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:52.700
And I find the whole practice very annoying.

255
00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:57.100
Because the people who do spray the seat, and most of the time they don't clean it up.

256
00:09:57.240 --> 00:09:57.400
No.

257
00:09:57.400 --> 00:10:00.500
And I really got upset about this when I was pregnant.

258
00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:05.180
Because, you know, when you definitely can't hover, when you've got a giant pregger's belly.

259
00:10:05.380 --> 00:10:05.660
Yeah.

260
00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:07.300
I got to sit down and go.

261
00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:10.200
But first, I'm cleaning up someone else's spray.

262
00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:12.440
Everyone, just sit down.

263
00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:13.440
Here's my situation.

264
00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:16.280
You know the little liner things that you can put?

265
00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:16.780
Yeah, the liner thing.

266
00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:17.240
Okay.

267
00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:22.800
